-<section class="language-section"><p class="eyebrow">One language, practical building blocks</p><h2>Build scripts. Services. AI workflows.</h2><div class="language-grid"><article><h3>Automate local work</h3><p>Work with files, processes, JSON and collections. Write small scripts with functions, structs and explicit error handling.</p><a href="/examples/files/">Explore file operations</a></article><article><h3>Build applications</h3><p>Use HTTP and networking helpers, modules, async tasks and generators. Check the documented runtime limits before choosing a deployment.</p><a href="/examples/http/">Inspect an HTTP server</a></article><article><h3>Make AI work inspectable</h3><p>Identify requests, replay recorded responses and validate structured data. Add packages when you need agent orchestration.</p><a href="/examples/replay/">Run an offline AI call</a></article></div></section>
-<section class="language-section"><p class="eyebrow">AI-native, with concrete mechanisms</p><h2>Repeat the request. Inspect the result.</h2><div class="language-grid"><article><h3>Stable request identity</h3><p><code>ai_request_hash</code> derives a deterministic request identifier while excluding credentials. Compare requests without sending them to a model.</p><a href="/examples/request-hash/">Run the hash example</a></article><article><h3>Offline model replay</h3><p><code>ai_chat</code> can read recorded cassettes. Reproduce a response in tests without a live provider, then validate its data with <code>json_schema_validate</code>.</p><a href="/examples/replay/">Replay a response</a> · <a href="/examples/structured-output/">Validate a schema</a></article></div><p>Conventional languages already offer async functions, HTTP clients and testing libraries. Kujo adds AI request identity, replay, streaming callbacks and capability controls at the runtime level. These mechanisms support review; they do not guarantee correctness or isolation.</p><p>Choose Kujo for scripts and tools that benefit from those primitives. Keep Python or TypeScript when existing libraries, deployment requirements or team experience matter more. Independent Kujo tools can still inspect and evaluate those repositories.</p><details><summary>What belongs to which layer?</summary><ul><li><strong>Language:</strong> functions, modules, collections, structs, enums, matching, async and generators.</li><li><strong>Rust VM/runtime:</strong> execution, host helpers, AI primitives and capability-aware access.</li><li><strong>Core CLI:</strong> check, format, lint, tests, LSP and documentation generation.</li><li><strong>Separate packages:</strong> Kennel, Agents SDK, AI SDK and workflow tools have their own installation and versions.</li><li><strong>External systems:</strong> live model providers require configuration and credentials; optional hosts and integrations are not bundled language features.</li></ul></details></section>
